 // v9.52 Windows-privesc source re-mining: the reviewed service/task/DLL/enumeration notes were re-read from the original private sources, which make trigger capability, ACL intent, and automated-tool trust an explicit, previously-unstated proof boundary. This is additive guidance on the existing Orange Windows local-privilege path node.
 Object.freeze({id:'note-windows-service-trigger-tool-proof',title:'A writable Windows service or task is only a lead until you can trigger it',body:'A writable service binary, an unquoted service-path candidate directory, a modifiable scheduled-task action, or a hijackable DLL search-path location is a lead, not elevation. Confirm the trigger you actually control: either the current user can restart the service or task (for example Start-Service and Stop-Service succeed), or the service is set to start automatically and the user holds the privilege needed to reboot (for example SeShutdownPrivilege in whoami /priv output). Read the object ACL yourself with icacls or Get-Acl: a write grant to a broad group such as Users, Everyone, or Authenticated Users is meaningful, but a grant marked inherited (I) is weaker evidence of intent than one set on the object directly. Treat automated privilege-escalation helpers such as PowerUp Get-ModifiableServiceFile, WinPEAS, and Seatbelt as leads only: they can be blocked by anti-virus and can miss or misreport a path you can actually write, so verify the writable-plus-triggerable chain by hand before recording elevated execution.',kind:'path-guidance',cardIds:freezeList([]),toolIds:freezeList(['powershell','icacls','winpeas']),pathIds:freezeList(['path']),tags:freezeList(['windows-privesc','windows-service','scheduled-task','dll-hijack','unquoted-path','permissions','proof-boundary']),sourceRefs:freezeList(['offsec-pen-200-ce579dd1e01f9b89','offsec-pen-200-035e996900a69250','offsec-pen-200-8d9c1610c3f9a5c2','offsec-pen-200-a30f08718703e1b1','offsec-pen-200-0cc4526b3d509ced'])})
